WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.930
Zara runs a fictional
research conservatory.

2
00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:08.100
One morning, an AI system rolls out
a long new manuscript,

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.870
with a surprising result.

4
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.500
It looks impressive.

5
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.800
But a result can be impressive before
anyone understands what it changes.

6
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:21.300
OpenAI recently reported research
manuscripts produced with its models.

7
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.670
It describes them as a
form of raw research data,

8
00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:28.150
which still has to be turned
into scientific understanding.

9
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.700
OpenAI says outside expert
groups are involved,

10
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.100
and that it is trying different
ways of releasing the work.

11
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.240
This film doesn’t judge those results.

12
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.720
It’s about what has to happen next.

13
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:44.451
In Zara’s conservatory, the manuscript
moves through three workbenches.

14
00:00:44.591 --> 00:00:47.001
The first checks the explicit steps.

15
00:00:47.001 --> 00:00:49.410
Does each one follow the stated rules?

16
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.240
Careful checking can confirm that,

17
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.986
but only for the rules
that were written down.

18
00:00:54.126 --> 00:00:56.596
The second examines the assumptions.

19
00:00:56.596 --> 00:00:58.316
What was taken for granted?

20
00:00:58.316 --> 00:01:01.243
Does it hold in the real
setting people care about?

21
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:03.793
The third explains the implications.

22
00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:07.533
What does this change, for whom,
and what does it leave unchanged?

23
00:01:07.673 --> 00:01:10.493
Here’s a simple puzzle card we invented.

24
00:01:10.493 --> 00:01:12.163
It isn’t a real paper.

25
00:01:12.163 --> 00:01:14.050
Every move follows the rules.

26
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.490
But the rules assume
the board never changes,

27
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.963
and checking the moves can’t
tell you whether that’s true.

28
00:01:20.103 --> 00:01:21.753
These are different tasks.

29
00:01:21.753 --> 00:01:25.093
A checked step is not the
same as an understood result.

30
00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:28.603
So when an AI result
arrives, ask three things.

31
00:01:28.603 --> 00:01:30.073
What exactly is claimed?

32
00:01:30.073 --> 00:01:31.273
What checks were done?

33
00:01:31.273 --> 00:01:33.043
And what remains uncertain?

34
00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:34.783
Impressive comes first.

35
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:38.864
Understanding takes people,
time and careful examination.

36
00:01:39.004 --> 00:01:40.474
Ahmadreza Samadi.

37
00:01:40.474 --> 00:01:42.411
More at a-samadi.com
